    "display_name": "Confirmed-Trend Filter on Concentrated-Manager Overweights",
    "discipline": "risk-managed",
    "thesis": "The angle is conviction weight: a manager's largest position by portfolio share is the one they were most willing to be publicly wrong about, and that only means something in a book small enough that a top position is a real bet rather than a rounding error -- which is why RENTECH (3,185 positions) and BRIDGEWATER (1,040) are excluded on their face and never queried. Universe was built from the two concentrated 13F managers whose disclosed holdings, as of the 2026-02-13 design cutoff, actually mapped to tradeable roster tickers with meaningful weight: PERSHING (period 2025-09-30, filed 2025-11-14, 11 positions -- Bill Ackman) and APPALOOSA (period 2025-09-30, filed 2025-11-13, 45 positions -- David Tepper). A holding qualifies only if its weight-within-that-manager's-book exceeds an equal-weight bar (1/11 for Pershing, 1/45 for Appaloosa) -- an objective 'this is actually overweighted' test, not a top-K pick. That leaves 8 names: AMZN and GOOGL from Pershing (2 of 11 raw positions mapped -- 9 lost, mostly UBER, BROOKFIELD, HOWARD HUGHES, RESTAURANT BRANDS, and a separate GOOG Class-C line the roster does not carry); AMZN, NVDA, TSM, META, MSFT, QCOM and AMD from Appaloosa (9 of 45 raw positions mapped -- 36 lost; MU and UNH also mapped but fell below Appaloosa's own 2.2% average-weight bar and were cut by the same rule that let the other seven in). Three other manager books were checked and contributed nothing. SCION (period 2025-09-30, filed 2025-11-03, 8 positions -- Michael Burry) mapped zero tradeable tickers, and more importantly its top four holdings by weight -- Palantir 66%, Nvidia 13.5%, Pfizer 11.1%, Halliburton 4.5%, together 95% of the disclosed book -- are PUT and CALL option lines, not equity shares; treating a Burry 13F 'top holding' as a bullish conviction pick would have been backwards, since the largest position was a bet against Palantir, not for it. ELLIOTT (period 2025-09-30, filed 2025-11-14, 35 positions -- Paul Singer) also mapped zero: only one line (EQIX) resolved a ticker at all and EQIX is not on the roster, and roughly 28% of the disclosed book by weight is itself index/sector-ETF puts and calls (QQQ, sector SPDRs, iShares) -- a hedge book, not a stock-picker's conviction list. BRK (period 2025-09-30, filed 2025-11-14, 41 positions -- Warren Buffett) mapped 3 tickers (GOOGL, AMZN, UNH) but all three sit below Berkshire's own 2.4% average-weight bar, so the same objective filter that built the rest of the universe excludes Berkshire entirely; its real largest position, Apple at 22.7% of the book, is not on the 43-symbol roster at all. At TRADE_START (2026-02-17) the surviving data is 95-96 days stale from filing and describes positions as they stood 140 days earlier; because this engine cannot rotate a universe mid-run, that snapshot is what the strategy trades for the full six-month window, aging to roughly nine months stale by 2026-08-18 with no way to see whether either manager has since exited. I tested whether the premise is even true in the one window I am allowed to look at: from 2025-11-14 (the latest of the two managers' filing dates -- the first day this information was actually public) through the 2026-02-13 cutoff, the equal-weighted 8-name conviction basket returned -3.93%, versus +10.13% for the equal-weighted 43-symbol trading roster and -1.13% for QQQM, a closer like-for-like large-cap-growth benchmark. Only 3 of 8 names (GOOGL +10.6%, TSM +28.6%, META +5.0%) beat QQQM over that window; AMZN, NVDA, MSFT, QCOM and AMD all lagged it, three of them (MSFT -21.3%, QCOM -19.1%, AMD -16.0%) by double digits. Over the only window I can check, buying what these managers were conviction-weighted into performed worse than an index benchmark and worse than doing nothing special -- that is folklore, not edge, and I am not building a buy-and-hold card on it. Instead the 13F data does exactly one job here: it picks the 8-name universe. Whether and when to actually own each name is decided by price alone, gated to require that the market has already been agreeing with the position over both an intermediate (50-day) and long (200-day) horizon, with RSI capped under 70 so entries are not chased into names already extended and already selling off the way MSFT, QCOM and AMD were doing for most of the measured window. That is the point of a risk-managed discipline applied to a signal I just measured as unreliable on its own: use it as a candidate list, not a buy trigger, and let price do the confirming. Sizing deliberately does not commit the whole book -- max_positions=6 of the 8 candidates at position_pct=0.15 (90% max exposure, a 10% cash buffer) -- because a risk-managed strategy built on a signal already shown to be weak should not also run fully invested. Stop and target are a fixed 8%/16% (2:1 planned reward:risk) with a 90-day max hold, chosen shorter than the full window specifically because the underlying 13F snapshot keeps aging every day the position is held; a trade that has not resolved in 90 days is running increasingly on stale institutional information regardless of what price is doing, so it is closed on a clock rather than held on the strength of a now much-older filing. On volatility: measured ATR(14)/close at the design cutoff clusters tightly for 7 of the 8 names (AMZN 4.2%, GOOGL 3.6%, NVDA 3.8%, TSM 4.6%, META 4.0%, MSFT 3.8%, QCOM 3.6%), so the single 8% stop sits at roughly 1.7-2.2x ATR for those seven -- a reasonable, not noise-width, distance. AMD is the exception at 7.1% ATR, nearly double the rest of the basket, so the same 8% stop is only about 1.1x AMD's ATR -- close to noise-width, and AMD is disproportionately likely to be stopped out on ordinary chop rather than a genuine trend break. AMD stays in the universe anyway, because it cleared the same overweight-vs-book-average bar as the other seven and cutting it after seeing its volatility would be tuning the universe to flatter the risk math rather than reporting the mismatch, which the brief asks for directly.",
